---
title: "The story — FrontierJS"
description: "A system you can hold in your head: data, access, integrations, background work, infrastructure, monitoring, testing, releases and tooling, each with one home and a small vocabulary."
render: static
---

<!--
  Every tool named here exists. A job with no tool yet is stated as a gap in
  its chapter rather than left out.
-->
<section id="top" class="hero">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">The story</p>
    <h1>A you can hold in your head.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Data, screens, access, email, background jobs, servers, monitoring, tests,
      deploys, backups. Each one usually brings its own tool, its own config file
      and a few words of its own. FrontierJS gives each one a home, and keeps
      the vocabulary small enough to carry.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapters">
  <div class="container">
    <ol class="arc">
      <li>
        <span class="n">00</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Why it stops fitting</h2>
          <p>Most apps say the same thing over and over. The schema says a Lead has an email. So do the validator, the TypeScript type, the access middleware and the API docs. That's five copies, and sooner or later two of them disagree.</p>
          <p>Worse, you have to keep all five in your head and guess which one is right. Add a job queue, a mail provider, a deploy script and a monitoring tool, each with its own config and its own words for things, and the picture stops fitting.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Every duplicated truth eventually becomes a duplicated mental model.</p>
          <p class="see">See: <a href="/before-after/">Before &amp; after</a></p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">01</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Start with what it knows</h2>
          <p>Before your app can do anything, it has to know something. What's a Lead? What fields does it have, what does it belong to, and what can't be left blank? You write that once, in <code>db/schema.lite</code>. Declare <code>model Lead</code> and <code>db.lead</code> already works. Litestone takes the tables, the types and the migrations from that one file.</p>
          <dl class="carry">
            <dt>Usually</dt><dd>an ORM schema, a set of types, a validator and a folder of migrations</dd>
            <dt>Here</dt><dd class="here">one file</dd>
          </dl>
          <p class="takeaway">Data is the foundation, not ORM boilerplate.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">02</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Three nouns</h2>
          <p>The app side of FrontierJS has three words. A <strong>Model</strong> is what the app knows. A <strong>Service</strong> is how it's reached: Junction serves <code>lead</code> as an API straight from the schema. A <strong>Resource</strong> is how it's shown: <code>Lead.mesa</code> builds its form from the same definition, and its list updates live over WebSocket.</p>
          <p>Need a marketing site, a widget, a browser extension, a desktop app or a CLI? Each one is a folder next to <code>api/</code> and <code>web/</code>, built from the same three nouns.</p>
          <dl class="carry">
            <dt>Usually</dt><dd>controllers, routes, DTOs, stores and components, each with its own idea of a Lead</dd>
            <dt>Here</dt><dd class="here">Model → Service → Resource</dd>
          </dl>
          <p class="takeaway">Learn three words and you can guess where anything lives.</p>
          <p class="predict">Predict it: why is this field a select?</p>
          <p class="see">See: <a href="/flow/">Flow</a></p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">03</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Rules in one place</h2>
          <p>Who's allowed to see a Lead? Edit one? Change its status? You answer that on the model itself: <code>@@gate</code> for who can read and write, <code>@encrypted</code> and <code>@guarded</code> for sensitive fields, and <code>@@transitions</code> for which status changes are allowed.</p>
          <p>Those rules are enforced at the data, so no route, job or agent can get around them. The UI can hide a button with <code>x-gate</code>, but the server checks either way. The audit trail logs protected fields as <code>[redacted]</code> without being asked, and <code>@frontierjs/auth</code> handles sign-in, sessions and API keys.</p>
          <dl class="carry">
            <dt>Usually</dt><dd>a permission check in every route, and the same check again in the UI</dd>
            <dt>Here</dt><dd class="here">the rules, on the model</dd>
          </dl>
          <p class="takeaway">Access is declared in the schema, not written into hooks.</p>
          <p class="predict">Predict it: who is allowed to delete a Lead?</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">04</span>
        <div>
          <h2>The outside world</h2>
          <p>Mail goes out, payments come in, and other systems want to hear when something changes. Every outside service you talk to is declared in one place, with its own timeouts and retries, and every call to it goes through <code>app.conduit.send()</code>. When something changes in your app, webhooks go out. They're delivered at least once and retried if the other end is down.</p>
          <dl class="carry">
            <dt>Usually</dt><dd>SDKs, keys and timeout settings scattered across the codebase</dd>
            <dt>Here</dt><dd class="here">one list of outside services, and one way to call them</dd>
          </dl>
          <p class="takeaway">Every outside service has one address in your code.</p>
          <p class="see">See: <a href="/seams/">Seams</a></p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">05</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Work nobody clicked</h2>
          <p>Some of the most important work happens when no one's clicking anything. Save a Lead and queue a follow-up in the same transaction, so the job only runs if the save really happened. Caravan runs the queue and the cron schedule, right on SQLite. A notification lands in the app, live on screen and in an inbox at once.</p>
          <p>An AI agent working through MCP gets exactly the permissions your gates already set, and nothing more. Orion, for triggers and automations, is being ported.</p>
          <dl class="carry">
            <dt>Usually</dt><dd>a second set of permission rules for workers, and a third for agents</dd>
            <dt>Here</dt><dd class="here">the same gates, everywhere</dd>
          </dl>
          <p class="takeaway">The same rules apply to work nobody clicked.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">06</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Where it runs</h2>
          <p>Your app has to live somewhere other than your laptop. Config differs per environment, secrets stay out of the repo, and every service needs a port. Config is read through <code>$.config</code>, or <code>app.configFor(tenant)</code> for a single tenant, and <code>fli env:*</code> moves environment values between machines. Ports follow a formula of environment, category, project and service, so dev, test and prod never collide and you can work out any port without looking it up.</p>
          <dl class="carry">
            <dt>Usually</dt><dd>a list of port numbers, and a guess about which <code>.env</code> is current</dd>
            <dt>Here</dt><dd class="here">a formula, and one place config is read</dd>
          </dl>
          <p class="takeaway">Where it runs is part of the design.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">07</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Keep watch</h2>
          <p>Every request carries a trace id. Health checks and metrics are registered right where the code lives, and the devtools panel shows calls as they happen. Outpost runs on each server, and Basecamp, itself a FrontierJS app, watches the whole fleet. <code>fli deploy:logs</code> is there when you need the raw output.</p>
          <dl class="carry">
            <dt>Usually</dt><dd>a dashboard for each tool, and a hunch about which one to check</dd>
            <dt>Here</dt><dd class="here">one fleet view, with health declared next to the code</dd>
          </dl>
          <p class="takeaway">Find out it's broken before a customer tells you.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">08</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Know what changed</h2>
          <p>Changing code shouldn't feel like defusing something. <code>createTestEnv</code> gives your tests a real database and a real API, built from the schema. Migrations come from diffing the schema. <code>fli test:access</code> writes your whole access policy out as a snapshot, so a change to who can do what shows up in the diff.</p>
          <p><code>fli proves</code> reads your change and names the checks it needs, and <code>fli done</code> tells you whether you're finished.</p>
          <dl class="carry">
            <dt>Usually</dt><dd>which tests cover what, and whether that last change touched permissions</dd>
            <dt>Here</dt><dd class="here">a diff that shows it, and a command that tells you what to run</dd>
          </dl>
          <p class="takeaway">You can see what a change does before it ships.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">09</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Ship and undo</h2>
          <p><code>fli release:mint</code> works out exactly what would deploy, without changing anything. <code>fli deploy</code> ships it, the deploy journal records every attempt, and <code>fli deploy:rollback</code> puts the previous release back.</p>
          <p><code>fli db:backup</code> takes a hot backup of every database the schema declares, and <code>fli db:import</code> pulls a copy of production down to your machine. One gap: restoring a backup into production is still a manual step.</p>
          <dl class="carry">
            <dt>Usually</dt><dd>the deploy steps, and what's actually live right now</dd>
            <dt>Here</dt><dd class="here">a Release, and a journal that remembers</dd>
          </dl>
          <p class="takeaway">Shipping is repeatable, and so is undoing it.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">10</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Tools that remember for you</h2>
          <p><code>fli check</code> turns your project's conventions into checks that fail, so you don't have to remember them. <code>fli</code> commands are written in markdown, so your project's own commands sit next to the framework's and you can read what they do. The editor extension, not yet published, understands <code>.lite</code> and <code>.mesa</code>. And an AI agent helping with your code reads the same map you do.</p>
          <dl class="carry">
            <dt>Usually</dt><dd>the conventions, in your head, until you forget one</dd>
            <dt>Here</dt><dd class="here">a check that remembers</dd>
          </dl>
          <p class="takeaway">Your tools follow the same rules as your app.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="reveal">
        <span class="n">11</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Count what you carry</h2>
          <p>Add it up. For the app: Model, Service and Resource. For shipping: a Release. For testing: a Suite. Under all of it sit three rules: <strong>one origin, one name, one owner.</strong> Every fact starts in one place, every thing has one name, and every job has one owner.</p>
          <p>Here's what that buys you. Take the <code>@@gate</code> on Lead. You wrote it once, and it shows up in five places:</p>
          <table class="views">
            <tbody>
              <tr><th>Data</th><td>the query refuses rows the caller can't see</td></tr>
              <tr><th>API</th><td>a denied call gets its status from the one place that maps errors</td></tr>
              <tr><th>UI</th><td><code>x-gate</code> hides the button, and the server enforces the rule either way</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Agent</th><td>the tool isn't listed for a caller who can't use it</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Proof</th><td><code>fli test:access</code> puts the change in the diff</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="takeaway">Predictability is the product.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">12</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Your head isn't the archive</h2>
          <p>Code tells you what. It doesn't tell you why. The reasons go in <code>DECISIONS.md</code>, what's broken goes in <code>ISSUES.md</code>, and what isn't built yet goes in <code>IDEAS/</code>. <code>fli</code> reads all three together: <code>fli next</code> suggests what to work on, <code>fli file</code> logs a new issue, and <code>fli decide</code> records a decision. Basecamp, the biggest app built on FrontierJS, is the worked example.</p>
          <dl class="carry">
            <dt>Usually</dt><dd>why things are the way they are, until you forget</dd>
            <dt>Here</dt><dd class="here">a record <code>fli</code> can read</dd>
          </dl>
          <p class="takeaway">The next person reading this might be you, six months from now.</p>
          <p class="see">See: <a href="/map/">Map</a> · <a href="/landscape/">Landscape</a> · <a href="/journey/">Journey</a></p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

